to construct.\n\nSet non-sunny lines: vertical x=1, vertical x=2, diagonal x+y=5. That's 3 non-sunny lines. But we need exactly 2 non-sunny lines (since k=2 sunny). So we cannot have 3 non-sunny lines. So we need to use only 2 non-sunny lines. Let's try to use vertical x=1 and diagonal x+y=5 as the two non-sunny lines. Then we have sunny lines: we need to cover remaining points not covered by those two lines.\n\nList points:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Vertical x=1 covers all of them.\n\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). Diagonal x+y=5 covers (2,3) (since 2+3=5). So (2,1) and (2,2) remain.\n\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2). Diagonal x+y=5 covers (3,2). So (3,1) remains.\n\nColumn 4: (4,1). Diagonal x+y=5 covers (4,1). So column 4 is covered.\n\nRemaining uncovered points: (2,1),(2,2),(3,1). That's three points. We have two sunny lines left. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So we could try to cover (2,1) and (3,1) with one sunny line? That would be horizontal line y=1, not sunny. So cannot. Could we cover (2,2) and (3,1) with one sunny line? Slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not sunny. Could we cover (2,1) and (2,2) with one sunny line? That's vertical, not sunny. So we cannot cover all three points with two sunny lines. So this configuration fails.\n\nTry different non-sunny lines: vertical x=1 and horizontal y=1. Then we have sunny lines to cover remaining points. Let's see.\n\nVertical x=1 covers column 1 (all four points). Horizontal y=1 covers (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1). So covered points: column 1 all, column 2 (2,1) covered, column 3 (3,1) covered, column 4 (4,1) covered. Remaining points: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4) are covered by vertical x=1, so okay. Actually vertical x=1 already covered (1,2),(1,3),(1,4). So all points in column 1 are covered. So remaining uncovered points: (2,2),(2,3),(3,2). Let's list all points:\n\n(1,1) covered by both.\n(1,2) covered by x=1.\n(1,3) covered by x=1.\n(1,4) covered by x=1.\n(2,1) covered by y=1.\n(2,2) not covered.\n(2,3)