r n=4, k=2 seems impossible.\n\nNow test n=5: T_5 points with a+b ≤6: columns: x=1 has 5 points, x=2 has 4, x=3 has 3, x=4 has 2, x=5 has 1. Total points = 5*6/2 = 15. Need 5 lines total, exactly 2 sunny lines, 3 non-sunny lines.\n\nWe need to see if any configuration exists. The solution's argument for k=2 uses column n-2 (size 3) requiring a vertical line, then column n-1 (size 2) requiring either vertical line or two sunny lines, and then point (n,1) requiring a third sunny line if we don't have vertical line x=n. But they didn't consider using vertical line x=n as a non-sunny line. However, they have only n-2 non-sunny lines total (since total lines n, sunny k=2). So they have n-2 non-sunny lines. For n≥3, n-2≥1. So they can use vertical lines for columns n-2 and n (maybe also n-1). But they need to allocate non-sunny lines to cover columns with many points. Let's examine the general case.\n\nWe have n columns: column i has h_i = n+1-i points. For i = n-2, h_{n-2}=3. For i = n-1, h_{n-1}=2. For i = n, h_n=1.\n\nWe have k=2 sunny lines. Each sunny line can intersect each column at most one point. So across all columns, the total number of points covered by sunny lines is at most 2 * n (since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once). But we also have non-sunny lines: n-2 of them. Non-sunny lines can be vertical (cover entire column), horizontal (cover many points across columns), or diagonal (cover many points across columns). The challenge is to cover all points.\n\nThe solution's argument: Since column n-2 has 3 points, we need at least one vertical line covering that column (since sunny lines cannot cover more than one point in that column). So we must have a vertical line x=n-2 among the non-sunny lines. That's fine.\n\nNow we have n-2 non-sunny lines total. One of them is used for column n-2. The remaining n-3 non-sunny lines can be used for other columns.\n\nNow consider column n-1 (size 2). If we do not have a vertical line x=n-1, then we nee