ng (1,2) and (1,3)? That's vertical line x=1, non-sunny. So not allowed. So we cannot cover (1,2),(1,3),(2,2) with two sunny lines. So horizontal y=1 fails.\n\nCase 5: Non-sunny line is horizontal y=2. Then points (1,2),(2,2) covered. Remaining points: (1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1). Columns: x=1 has two points (1,1),(1,3); x=2 has one point (2,1); x=3 has one point (3,1). Two sunny lines can cover at most two points in column 1, which is okay. Let's try to cover (1,1) and (2,1) with a sunny line? That's slope (1-1)/(2-1) = 0, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we cover (1,1) and (3,1)? That's horizontal line y=1, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we cover (1,3) and (2,1)? Slope (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, sunny. So line L1: through (1,3) and (2,1) slope -2. That covers (1,3) and (2,1). Then we need to cover (1,1) and (3,1) with another sunny line. That's horizontal line y=1, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we cover (1,1) and (3,1) with a sunny line? No. Could we cover (1,1) and (2,1) with a sunny line? No. Could we cover (1,1) and (1,3) with a sunny line? That's vertical line x=1, non-sunny. So fails.\n\nCase 6: Non-sunny line is horizontal y=3. Then points (1,3) covered. Remaining points: (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1). Columns: x=1 has two points (1,1),(1,2); x=2 has two points (2,1),(2,2); x=3 has one point (3,1). Two sunny lines can cover at most two points in column 1 (one per line) and two points in column 2 (one per line). So we could assign each sunny line to cover one point in column 1 and one point in column 2, and maybe also cover (3,1). Let's try to find two sunny lines that cover all remaining points. Suppose L1 passes through (1,1) and (2,2). That's slope (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, sunny. L1 also passes through (3,3) not needed. L2 passes through (1,2) and (2,1). That's slope (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we choose L2 to pass through (1,2) and (3,1)? Slope (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, sunny. L2: line through (1,2) and (3,1). That covers (1,2) and (3,1). So we have L1: 